% This report provides an actionable framework for securing agentic AI systems in realistic deployment environments. We argue that safety and security are not just fixed attributes of individual models but emergent properties arising from the dynamic interactions among models, orchestrators, tools, and data in their operating environments. Building on this foundation, we define an operational agentic risk taxonomy that unifies traditional concerns such as content safety and PII leakage with novel, uniquely agentic risks, including tool misuse, cascading action chains, and unintended control amplification. We then propose a layered and compositional agentic risk management strategy that embeds safety and security principles at every stage of the agentic development lifecycle, ranging from policy and design to pre-deployment and post-deployment monitoring. At its core, we introduce our dynamic agentic safety and security framework that operationalizes contextual agentic risk management by using auxiliary AI models and agents, under human oversight, to autonomously assist in contextual risk discovery, evaluation, and mitigation.
